+		// MU2_BGFX: store actions that keep tile memory on the tile. Apple's GPUs render a
+		// pass in on-chip tiles and write each attachment back to memory at the end of it,
+		// and bgfx asked for every write back: all four samples of an MSAA colour beside its
+		// resolve, and every depth. When no later pass this frame loads an attachment again,
+		// that write is pure bandwidth -- at 2560x1440 with 4x MSAA about 118 MB for an
+		// RGBA16F colour and 59 MB for a D32F depth. The frame is planned once from its sort
+		// keys before it is drawn: which segments (a view's compute or its draws) open a new
+		// render pass, and the last such pass that binds each texture. A pass then drops the
+		// samples of a resolved MSAA colour, and the contents of a write-only depth, that
+		// nothing after it loads. Only those two: anything else may be sampled later, and
+		// sampling is not visible in the plan. A pass opened where the plan did not expect
+		// one turns the whole thing off for the rest of the run and says so once.
+		enum { kMaxPlanSegments = BGFX_CONFIG_MAX_VIEWS * 2 };
